## 1) Python Scripting Mindset

Python scripting means writing small, focused programs that automate a repeatable task: moving files, transforming data, calling an API, checking system health, generating reports, or connecting systems.

### What makes a good script?
- It has one clear responsibility.
- It accepts input instead of relying on values hidden in the code.
- It validates assumptions and fails with a useful message.
- It logs important actions and errors.
- It is safe to run repeatedly when possible (idempotent).
- It does not expose passwords, tokens, or personal data.
- It returns a meaningful exit code for automation tools.
- It is easy to test without changing production data.

### Script vs application
| Script | Application |
|---|---|
| Usually focused on one operational task | Usually provides a long-running service or user interface |
| Often run manually, by a scheduler, or in CI/CD | Usually has defined deployment and lifecycle management |
| Commonly uses the standard library | May use frameworks and several external services |
| Still needs validation, logging, tests, and security | Needs those concerns at a larger scale |

